const HTML = "<p>I was fifteen when the doctor told me I had cancer.</p>\n<p>I watched his mouth form the word and waited for a reaction. I understood what he was saying and knew people died of cancer, but I felt no more than if he had read out a McDonald&#39;s menu. I remember the strangeness of that more clearly than anything I might have said.</p>\n<p>My parents took in the news while I sat there. I don&#39;t know whether being young helped me or whether I simply couldn&#39;t understand that this was happening to me, but I didn&#39;t cry and I don&#39;t remember asking whether I would die.</p>\n<p>Until then, I had been given very little reason to feel unsafe. My father was steady in a way I took for granted, his strength so apparent that he didn&#39;t need to make speeches about it. His word held, and I trusted him. My mother made home a place I wanted to return to. I remember coffee in the afternoon, music while dinner cooked, her hand on my shoulder as she passed. I could try something, fail at it and come home, and nothing in those ordinary arrangements had suggested they might not last.</p>\n<p>Within days I was in hospital with tubes taped to my skin. I remember a doctor explaining that they would calculate what would kill me, then reduce it a little. Whatever else I understood about the treatment, it was clear that it was going to hurt me too.</p>\n<p>My life acquired a three-week rhythm: a week of treatment, a week when I couldn&#39;t eat, then a week when I ate as much as I could before we began again. Food I had liked became so disgusting that sometimes the smell was enough, and even water could be difficult. Then hunger returned and I could put a little weight back on, until my body began to feel more like mine and it was time to return to the hospital.</p>\n<p>There was not much room for philosophy in this. Getting through an hour could occupy me completely, and I was more concerned with what I could swallow than with the fact that I might die.</p>\n<p>The other children were harder to keep out of my thoughts. There was a playroom on the ward where I saw them with their IV stands, some much younger than me, though I rarely spoke to them. I think I was ashamed of being ill, without being able to say exactly what the shame was about. Being seen without my hair, weakened and attached to a machine, meant getting more attention than I wanted at fifteen. I kept to myself.</p>\n<p>Some children died.</p>\n<p>I would return and a bed that had held a child would be empty, with fresh sheets and no parent sitting beside it. I knew what had happened, but I could only think about it briefly. There was the empty bed, and there was everything it might mean for me. I would look away.</p>\n<p>My friends still had school, crushes, arguments and parties. I don&#39;t blame them for living their lives; I was their age too, although I was learning which blood counts had to improve before I could go home. I had assumed that death belonged much later, and the ward gave me no way to keep believing that.</p>\n<p>I survived.</p>\n<p>My hair grew back, I returned to school, and ordinary life gradually began requiring things of me again. I was glad to be well enough that people could expect me to do more than endure treatment.</p>\n<p>But my family hadn&#39;t been waiting unchanged for me to recover. My illness had put pressure on my parents&#39; marriage, and although they loved me and loved each other, fear and exhaustion had made the house harder to live in. Conversations became sharper and silences lasted. The safety I remembered from before the diagnosis did not return as neatly as my hair did.</p>\n<p>More people died in the years that followed, and I found myself in hospital corridors again, this time beside someone else&#39;s bed. I had known what it was to hold still for a needle or swallow a pill. Beside a person I loved, there was so little to do: adjust a blanket, offer water, touch an arm. I could be physically well and strong and have no useful way to spend any of it.</p>\n<p>I began thinking about my own death at night. The loss I couldn&#39;t accept was not a particular pleasure or ambition but all of it: the memories, the people I loved, the private jokes that required no explanation because the right person was there. The world would carry on without any need for me to witness it, with people buying groceries and complaining about traffic. That continuation was perfectly reasonable, and I resented it.</p>\n<p>I tried to imagine being absent from it. Each attempt placed me somewhere nearby, still able to notice what I was missing, as though nonexistence were a place where I would be left alone with the knowledge that everyone else was alive. I could frighten myself very effectively with that picture.</p>\n<p>Religion did not give me an answer I could be sure of, and philosophy gave me things to consider but no means of checking what happened after death. I kept trying to supply an experience where I had no experience to draw on.</p>\n<p>But if consciousness ends when I die, I won&#39;t remain somewhere missing my life. There will be no one left to experience the absence. I could say that in a sentence long before I found much comfort in it, because the fear was happening now, to someone very much alive, and it did not immediately yield to a better explanation.</p>\n<p>Over time, though, that thought helped. I was no longer quite so convinced that dying meant being shut out of life while remaining conscious enough to suffer the exclusion; the part that would suffer belonged to the life that had ended.</p>\n<p>It also changed what I understood death to end. I had been thinking almost exclusively about what I would want to keep, although there is plenty I wouldn&#39;t: pain, anxiety, regret, the return of a memory I would rather have left alone. If death ends experience, it ends those too. I couldn&#39;t count the loss of every pleasure while imagining that the capacity to suffer would somehow remain.</p>\n<p>I don&#39;t offer this as consolation for the death of a child. Those children should have had the time I have had. I wouldn&#39;t tell their parents to regard the loss as merciful, and I would not have wanted anybody to say it to mine. I am trying to describe a change in how I think about my own eventual death, after years of finding the thought almost impossible to bear.</p>\n<p>I still want to live. I expect that when I am dying I will want more time, and I may be frightened regardless of what I have worked out while healthy. Understanding something in a quiet room is not the same as living through it in a hospital bed. I know at least that much from being fifteen.</p>\n<p>But death no longer hits the same. If there is something beyond it, I don&#39;t know what it is; if there is nothing, I won&#39;t be there to regret anything. If there&#39;s something, then I know I can stand by my choices. Regardless, I know that when that time comes, my questions will be finally answered, or finally forgotten.</p>\n";

